Joaquin Menchaca wrote:
> ...I think NuKernel of Copland is a better choice as it combines the
> best of Apple with the best of NeXT (OPENSTEP).

I doubt this.  Time is Apple's most precious resource, and that
choice would have added a lot of work to the schedule.

> Playing devil's advocate:
> For the business side, this could be the correct decision.  Apple needs to
> ship a developement system hosting NeXT for developers.  This needs to be
> done as soon as possible.  Afterwards, the kernel may have more features,
> but the monolithic kernel was probaly chosen in order to have a little
> risks and be able to meet deadlines.  This is a sound decision.

I think the division between "microkernel" and "monolithic" is very
much less clear than you think it is.

It's possible to have a true microkernel where all of the facilities
are properly abstracted -- and then decide (at compile time) that
a stable single-server can be moved into the kernel space for
performance reasons.  The actual code for the kernel doesn't change
much at all -- most everything has the same logical structure,
yet people could very well call it "monolithic" even when they've
never seen the source code.

So, you're right that Apple needs to ship something now, but
I think you're overblowing the magnitude of the "mistake" 
of monolithicity.

> Afterwards, there is no reason, Apple could upgrade their monolithic
> kernel (Mach 2.5++) to that of a better kernel like good microkernel
> and/or a distributed cluster based kernel to the highend publishing,
> server, and rendering markets.

Yes -- probably quite painlessly -- and with relatively few
changes to the kernel source!

