 Brian J. Krupski <krupski@idx.com> wrote:
}I am launching a C program from a bourne shell script and trying to exit
}the C program with an exit() status so that the bourne script can
}interpret the exit status and take the appropriate action.  However, it
}only seems to work when I a exit(0).  If I try to exit with a number
}less than or greater than 0 the script interprets it as 255.
}The code follows:
}      $program >/dev/null 2>&1
}      status="$?"
}      echo $status		255 is returned on this line.
}Any ideas????

Here's an example which works, perhaps it will be some help:

eeyore.cc> cat > exit.c
main() { exit(17); }                      /* exit(0..255) */
eeyore.cc> cc -o exit exit.c
eeyore.cc> cat > exit.sh
#!/bin/sh
./exit
status="$?"
echo $status
eeyore.cc> chmod +x exit.sh exit.csh
eeyore.cc> ./exit.sh
17


John
